              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Run</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10–12 mph, a belt of 55 in. or longer, powered incline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Aeriflo X-T510A</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Jog and walk</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">8–10 mph, powered incline, 16 in. or wider belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Ksports 16.5-inch, LifeSmart AT1 Pro</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walk at a desk</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Low profile, quiet, incline for effort</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">WELLFIT 12% Walking Pad</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walk with support</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Long side handrails, low top speed is fine</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Ksports Advanced Rehab, SereneLife</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Runners should take belt length seriously. A 47-inch belt, like the LifeSmart&rsquo;s, suits
            walking and easy jogging; at a real running stride most people want 55 to 60 inches. Our <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt size guide</a> gives lengths by height and speed. Walkers, on the other hand, gain most from incline: a route
            video of a mountain pass feels more real when the deck tilts with it, which is another reason
            to prefer powered incline. See our <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best incline treadmills</a> for machines that go steeper, and <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills with decline</a> if you want the downhill sections too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Connect a Treadmill to Kinomap</h2>
<p>
            The exact screens change between app versions, but the sequence is the same on every
            treadmill here:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Switch the treadmill on and insert the safety key.</strong> Many treadmills only
            advertise over Bluetooth once the console is awake.</li>
            <li><strong>Close the treadmill&rsquo;s own app</strong> (FitShow or the maker&rsquo;s) on
            every phone in the room. A treadmill usually accepts one Bluetooth app connection at a time,
            and a second phone that connected first will block Kinomap.</li>
            <li><strong>In Kinomap, add equipment</strong>, choose treadmill, and pick your treadmill from
            the list of devices it finds. Do not pair it in the phone&rsquo;s system Bluetooth settings
            first; training apps generally connect from inside the app.</li>
            <li><strong>Start slowly.</strong> Begin a route at a walking pace and check that the video
            follows your speed. If the app is meant to control incline, watch the first climb with your
            hands near the rails.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            If the treadmill does not appear, restart both the treadmill and the phone, and check the
            Kinomap equipment list for the model. A treadmill whose speed reads wrongly in the app may
            need calibrating; our <a href="/treadmill-calibration/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill calibration guide</a> explains how to check it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>4 Mistakes People Make Buying a Kinomap Treadmill</h2>
          <p>Most of these come from reading “compatible” as more than it promises.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Expecting the app to change the incline</h3>
              <p>App-controlled hills need powered incline and a treadmill that accepts control over Bluetooth. A manual-incline treadmill, or one that only sends data, will play the route but leave the slope to you. Check Kinomap’s equipment list before buying for this feature.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a short belt for running</h3>
              <p>A walking pad or 47-inch belt with a 7.5 mph top speed is a walking machine. If you plan to run Kinomap routes, look for about 55 to 60 inches of belt and at least 10 mph, as on the Aeriflo.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring a missing weight capacity</h3>
              <p>Two picks here do not state a maximum user weight on their listings. Ask the seller before ordering if you are near or above 250 lb, and leave a margin under whatever figure you are given.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Leaving the maker’s app connected</h3>
              <p>Most treadmills accept one Bluetooth app at a time. If FitShow or the maker’s app is open on any phone in the room, Kinomap may not find the treadmill. Close it on every device before pairing.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

